## v0.25.11

### Ops tooling: rotation safety gates, admin-key file source, census tool

- `f3a54237d6` — `governance_parameter_change.py` funded-authority gate: `escrow_settlement_authority` and `bond_slash_authority` pay their own transaction fees after rotation, so sign-submit now refuses a target below the fee+headroom floor unless `--allow-unfunded-target` is passed deliberately (the failure mode seen when the settlement authority rotated to an address with no account row). `plan` prints each target's balance. `bridge_release_authority` (in-state credits, no account fees) and `escrow_fee_recipient` (receives only) are not gated.
- `49d8cc159e` — `register_bridge_validators.py` gains `BRIDGE_ADMIN_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` — a path to a file holding the admin key (the workstation-custody `keys/bridge_admin.key` created for the dedicated bridge-admin address). A file key is single-source, so it is literal-class: `BRIDGE_ADMIN_ADDRESS` stays optional but is enforced when declared; `*_SOURCE` indirection still requires the binding declaration. `BRIDGE_ADMIN_ADDRESSES` service-side is untouched.
- `220d8f303a` — `scripts/ops/key_residue_census.py`: read-only scanner for the KR-7 residue census — walks files/units/scripts, reports file/line/variable plus *which retired address* matched (never the value), and derives the public address of every 64-hex key literal or `*_PRIVATE_KEY` value to report only the derived address and whether it is retired. Refuses out-of-root symlinks, skips binary files, reports file modes, exits non-zero on a retired hit, writes nothing.
- `9b9c4577c4` (unpushed) — `scripts/ops/watch_drift_check.py`: compares every bound `AITBC_WATCH_ACCOUNTS` extras entry with the address derived from its env-held key (`governance_proposer` ← `GOVERNANCE_PROPOSER_PRIVATE_KEY` in `aitbc-governance.env`, `validator_hub` ← `PROPOSER_KEY` in `validator-secrets.env`), prints OK|MISMATCH|ERROR|SKIP per role, exits 1 on drift. The follow-on check for the extras entries above: a rotated key that forgets to move its watch entry is caught instead of going blind.


### WEB-FIX-1 follow-ups: live-verified residual + MSG-FIX-1b NUL gate

- `0906c8376` — live 375px verification after the hub pull caught a residual overflow on `alerts.html` (412px): `.journal-controls` and its selects each floored at `min-width:auto` min-content (long unit names), which beats `max-width:100%`. `min-width:0` on both layers; `test_w2_fix_pinned` now pins it and the journal fixture carries a real unit name so the browser test exercises the regression path.
- `8458e575e`/`f416e45d4` — restamps; current common stamp `v=a324edd785f944ca`.
- `7bd725106` (MSG-FIX-1b) — director-found hole in the F1 SQL narrowing: `length()`/`GLOB`/`substr`/`instr` stop at embedded NUL, so `a\x00<junk>` topics passed every check and could mint topic names in the pure-SQL `topics`/`state` aggregates (row reads were always safe — the Python predicate still runs). Gate: `length(json) == length(CAST AS BLOB)` — byte length equals char length exactly for NUL-free ASCII; non-ASCII is already rejected by the charset glob.


### MSG-TEST-WAIT-1

- tests/cli/test_commands_messaging.py: rewrote test_send_waits_for_seal for the MSG-CLI-WAIT poll path (requests.get -> /rpc/messaging/messages/<hash>, 200 + block_height asserts 'Sealed at block 42'); autouse _no_real_http fixture makes the file network-proof. The red test reached main because no pre-commit/pre-push hook runs pytest and tests/cli only runs in GitHub CI (make test-cli), which gitea pushes never trigger.


### TEST-1: hook interpreter resolution + test-env isolation (2026-10-15)

Six 15-Oct gap-log rows, all repo tooling or test infrastructure — no
service runtime behaviour changes.

- **Shared repo-python resolver (gap rows 27/52).**
  `scripts/ci/repo-python.sh` is the single resolver for hook/CI
  scripts: `$PYTHON` env (validated — must exist and run a trivial
  import — then trusted), `<checkout>/venv/bin/python`, then the main
  checkout's venv located through `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, so a
  linked worktree still finds the reference venv. No `python3`
  fallback: a dependency-poor interpreter turned "ran" into "checked
  nothing" (`python3 -m ruff` absent → empty scan → green-looking skip).
  `PYTHON=python3` stays the explicit opt-in. Failures exit 1 naming
  `PYTHON=<interpreter>`. Wired into all eight scripts with the defect
  class: check-mypy-changed.sh (was a hardcoded `venv/bin/python` —
  reported bogus type errors on new files), check-c901-ratchet.sh,
  check_deprecation_cleanup.sh, check-mcp-cli-drift.sh,
  check-cli-docs.sh, check-except-ratchet.sh, check-openapi-drift.sh,
  mypy-precommit.sh. Before/after in a venv-less worktree:
  check-mypy-changed failed a clean new file with "Type errors"; after,
  real mypy runs and passes it. Surfaced backlog, pre-existing and now
  finally visible: check-except-ratchet defaulted to python3
  *everywhere*, so its baseline drifted ~52 files while the gate ran an
  empty scan — `make except-ratchet`/`make ci` report it now.
- **MCP drift + generator format (row 36).** The drift check formats
  via `"$PYTHON" -m ruff` — the pinned venv ruff — instead of a
  version-skewed PATH ruff (0.16 vs 0.15 produced false 76-file drift);
  `generate_mcp_cli_tools.py` ruff-formats its own output so a plain
  regen is drift-clean in one step, and the printed `Run:` hint names
  the resolved interpreter.
- **Wallet suite env isolation (rows 54/55).**
  `apps/wallet/tests/conftest.py` module-top: a guard raises at
  collection if `aitbc.constants`/`wallet_app.settings`/`ApiKeyStorage`
  were already bound outside a test scratch (combined runs fail loudly
  instead of leaking — observed firing on a `tests/unit`-first run
  where constants had bound `CONFIG_DIR=/etc/aitbc`,
  `LOG_DIR=/var/log/aitbc`); ambient env Settings/DatabaseConfig parse
  is popped (DEBUG, APP_*/ENVIRONMENT/NODE_ENV, HOST/PORT, DATABASE_URL
  family, AITBC_HOME); AITBC_DATA_DIR/CONFIG_DIR/LOG_DIR and
  API_KEY_STORAGE_PATH are force-assigned to a `tempfile.mkdtemp`
  scratch cleaned at atexit. 303 wallet tests pass under `env -i` with
  DEBUG=release, `/nonexistent` marker paths and a postgres
  DATABASE_URL; nothing touches the markers.
- **Adapter contract + uvicorn pin (row 56).**
  `test_adapter_provides_handler_contract_attributes` asserts the real
  FastAPIRequestAdapter exposes every attribute handlers read off self
  (client_address, headers, path, command, request, rfile/wfile) —
  mutation-verified, deleting the client_address line fails it.
  `test_server_runs_uvicorn_without_proxy_headers` spies uvicorn.run in
  server.main() and pins `proxy_headers=False`, which TestClient
  bypasses.

`tests/unit/test_repo_python.py` covers all resolver branches including
a real linked worktree and a dangling venv symlink.

Commits: `af1b2adaa` (resolver + 8 hook scripts + generator format +
resolver tests), `d75f144d4` (wallet conftest isolation), `6d0d57b8e`
(exchange contract/pin tests).


## v0.25.9

### governance_executors gate verified and pinned (2026-09-29)

Operator-ordered check, outcome: **no emergency execute needed** —
``governance_executors`` is set on all five hosts
(``0x02B8F2C61DB19B04aB68cfb43d0605E63dE74c5B``, applied at block 9819 via
``gap56-set-executors``; identical values verified by direct query and the
digest monitor).

Empirical gate verification (in-memory harness on node2):

- funded non-executor ``GOVERNANCE_EXECUTE`` (parameter_change →
  bridge_release_authority=attacker): REJECTED at v5/v6/v7/v8 —
  "not an authorized executor".
- executor sender: passes.
- executor list UNSET: REJECTED at v5+ ("parameter is not set" — the
  fail-closed branch). Lenient only at v4, where sealed history predates
  the parameter — the intended compatibility path.
- chain_parameter_history audit: 4 authority-parameter changes total, all
  gap56–gap60 proposals to known addresses — every change accounted for.

New regression test ``tests/test_governance_executor_gate.py`` (4 cases)
pins the gate so it cannot silently regress.

Queued version-height work (unchanged): bind ``execution_payload`` to the
stored proposal rather than the tx-carried override, and — if on-chain
governance is intended — signed votes with stake-snapshot tallies gating
execution. The executor key remains the most sensitive key: it can
reassign every authority, including itself — first in the key-split list.

### feat(v9): shadow-window positive control + scripted pin gate

- v9_signature_verdict now counts every evaluation into v9_shadow_checked_total plus per-type v9_shadow_checked series; both the checked aggregate and v9_would_reject_total are pre-created at import. A clean shadow window is provably checked>0 AND would_reject==0 instead of relying on a lazily-created series being absent.
- fleet-config-check gains a "v9 shadow window (pin gate)" section: the fleet window is bounded by the latest validator restart, reports per-host checked/reject and the per-type traffic mix, and prints WINDOW elapsed-vs-24h so the pin gate is a command output, not manual judgement.
- NOTE: v9_policy.py is chain code — it must ride the pin deploy; a fleet restart inside the window would restart the window. A checkout pull is inert (running processes keep loaded code); do NOT restart aitbc-blockchain-node until the pin.


### fix(v9): bound shadow-metric cardinality; pin gate checks the _other series

- The per-type `v9_shadow_checked_<type>_total` series are clamped to the known transaction-type allowlist — an unknown or malformed `tx_type` can no longer mint unbounded Prometheus label series (`091ca4c`). The shadow-window bound is anchored to validator restarts only.
- `IPFS_SUBSCRIPTION` joined `V9_METRIC_KNOWN_TX_TYPES` — it was landing in `_other` despite being a legitimate type. New `test_known_metric_types_cover_apply_path` derives the apply-recognized type set mechanically and fails on drift (`7fdf202`).
- The fleet-check pin gate prints `v9_shadow_checked_other_total` per host and flags any non-zero value — a clean window requires it to be 0 or explained on record (`22a486d`).
- Restart epochs are read via `ActiveEnterTimestamp` + `date -d` — the fleet's systemd build lacks USec (`f120261`).


